WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.729 --> 00:00:03.580
This is very interesting to see.

00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:10.169
Republican Senator and Trump brownnoser Rand
Paul went on Fox News this weekend and tried

00:00:10.169 --> 00:00:12.980
to pawn off on Fox News host Chris Wallace.

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:19.350
This silly notion that Donald Trump can't
be tried for his impeachment after being out

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:20.350
of office.

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:25.760
Now, remember that even though legally Trump
can be impeached after leaving office, he

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:28.050
was impeached while in office.

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:30.029
But the trial is happening afterwards.

00:00:30.029 --> 00:00:31.419
It's happening now.

00:00:31.419 --> 00:00:33.270
There's nothing unconstitutional about this.

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:36.910
There's really nothing particularly remarkable,
remarkable about that.

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:43.129
But Rand Paul tries that failed argument on
Chris Wallace and Chris Wallace shuts it down

00:00:43.129 --> 00:00:44.129
and rightly so.

00:00:44.129 --> 00:00:45.129
Let's look at the first clip.

00:00:45.129 --> 00:00:51.530
The other side argues that under that standard,
a president could abuse his power, quit at

00:00:51.530 --> 00:00:56.350
the last minute and could not be held accountable
by the Senate for that.

00:00:56.350 --> 00:00:59.640
Obviously, if it's if it's a legal crime,
he could be.

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:05.150
But for a political crime, the Senate would
be unable to touch him under your standard.

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:08.720
I think that argument ignores the fact that,
yes, the president can be prosecuted.

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:13.980
And if they think he is inciting a riot or
an insurrection, which is such an overstatement

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:19.310
and such a conclusion without any sort of
presentation of evidence, all the left wing

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:20.310
media just concludes it.

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:22.400
But the thing is, is yes, they can take him
to court.

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:24.010
They can take him to trial and do it.

00:01:24.010 --> 00:01:26.250
I think he has suffered under public opinion.

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:27.590
His numbers are greatly reduced.

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:29.660
And so I think there's all kinds of punishment.

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:33.460
But we have to look, we're not a country for
two hundred and fifty years.

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:38.920
And every other Congress thought it was unwise
to keep, you know, going after an ex-president.

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:42.930
So that's actually super slippery of Rand
Paul.

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:45.950
Rand Paul throws together a bunch of different
arguments there.

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:51.080
Now, they're all bad arguments, but when you
put them all together, it sounds sort of like,

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:56.920
wow, that that was a lot of arguments, lots
of reasons why Trump can't be tried now.

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:58.790
But don't let them Gish Gallop you.

00:01:58.790 --> 00:02:01.380
Let's actually think through each of the arguments
first.

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:09.399
Rand Paul says if Trump incited if Trump incited
an insurrection, there can be legal repercussions.

00:02:09.399 --> 00:02:11.330
Trump could be charged criminally.

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:13.250
Trump could be prosecuted criminally.

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:14.250
He could be convicted.

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:15.939
He could be sentenced criminally, criminally.

00:02:15.939 --> 00:02:20.510
Now, that's, first of all, a red herring because
it has nothing to do with the House and Senate.

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:22.480
The House and Senate don't have that power.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:27.060
The House and Senate don't have the responsibility
of being involved in the criminal prosecution

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:28.060
of a president.

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:33.390
The House looks at actions and asks, did it
meet the threshold for an impeachment?

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:35.599
If the answer is yes, then they impeach.

00:02:35.599 --> 00:02:38.659
And then the Senate holds its trial and evaluates
the evidence.

00:02:38.659 --> 00:02:42.659
Law enforcement can do whatever it wants,
but it has nothing to do with the House and

00:02:42.659 --> 00:02:43.659
Senate.

00:02:43.659 --> 00:02:44.659
So it's a red herring.

00:02:44.659 --> 00:02:50.290
But Rand Paul also wants to have it both ways
because it's been these same Republicans arguing

00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:51.290
for more than.

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:55.189
Four years that you can't charge a president
for crimes committed while in office, it's

00:02:55.189 --> 00:02:58.709
sort of like a if the president does it, it's
not a legal defense.

00:02:58.709 --> 00:03:01.579
And now Rand Paul wants it both ways.

00:03:01.579 --> 00:03:08.410
Then Rand says Trump has suffered enough because
his approval is down as he left office.

00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:10.150
That's also a non argument.

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:14.010
It's like being saying, listen, the crimes
you committed were embarrassing.

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:15.950
So we're not going to prosecute you.

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:16.950
Doesn't work that way.

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:19.329
So it's another distraction.

00:03:19.329 --> 00:03:26.260
And then Rand Paul says in the past, Congress
chose not to impeach former presidents.

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:31.900
But again, it's factually wrong since Trump
was impeached while in office.

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:34.900
It's the trial that's happening now.

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:37.689
And it doesn't matter that that's how it was
done in the past.

00:03:37.689 --> 00:03:43.609
The fact that in the past choices were made
one way doesn't mean it's unconstitutional

00:03:43.609 --> 00:03:44.650
to do it differently today.

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:46.590
And that was his main thing, that it's unconstitutional.

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:49.160
So Rand Paul moving the goalposts.

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:52.109
The question was about the constitutionality.

00:03:52.109 --> 00:03:56.650
And now he says, well, Trump's already been
embarrassed and you could charge him criminally.

00:03:56.650 --> 00:03:58.950
And in the past, we didn't do it this way.

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:00.800
None of that is about constitutionality.

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:02.060
Let's look at a little more.

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:05.340
And here, Chris Wallace, just shut it down.

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:11.310
Constitutional scholars have said that the
Constitution says you impeach and disqualify.

00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:14.560
If the person isn't there to impeach, you
can't do either one of them.

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:16.720
And so there are constitutional experts.

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:20.239
Senator Paul, also before my.

00:04:20.239 --> 00:04:21.290
That's not quite true.

00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:22.940
If I if I may if I may.

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:30.200
In 1876, the then secretary of war, William
Belknap, serving under Ulysses S. Grant, was

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:33.060
about to be impeached for accepting bribes.

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:37.460
He raced to the White House and resigned,
but the House impeached him anyway, and the

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:42.135
Senate decided that it had the authority to
try him and it didn't try them on.

00:04:42.135 --> 00:04:46.640
Although a majority sought to remove him to
convict him, they didn't get the two thirds.

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:50.580
But isn't that the 1876 case of William Belknap?

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:52.560
A solid precedent?

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:57.030
No, because we've never impeach the president,
because we always thought it would destroy

00:04:57.030 --> 00:04:59.670
and tear up the country and divide us further.

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:02.750
So that's another slippery attempt from Rand
Paul.

00:05:02.750 --> 00:05:10.670
Chris Wallace says, Isn't my example an example
of how this clearly is constitutional?

00:05:10.670 --> 00:05:17.020
And Rand says no, because that wasn't a president
and doing it to a president would divide the

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:18.280
country.

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:23.140
Why are we talking about dividing the country
if the initial argument Rand Paul was making

00:05:23.140 --> 00:05:24.780
was about constitutionality?

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:25.910
It's completely pathetic.

00:05:25.910 --> 00:05:27.800
But what is what is sad?

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:33.590
You know, I know I've said this before, but
Trump saying I'm going to run for president.

00:05:33.590 --> 00:05:38.990
Wacky people choose to run for president all
the time in many countries.

00:05:38.990 --> 00:05:41.120
That's not really the problem.

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:48.460
The problem was that in the Republican Party
primary electorate, you had so many people

00:05:48.460 --> 00:05:50.160
willing to vote for Donald Trump.

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:51.970
That was really the problem.

00:05:51.970 --> 00:05:55.520
And if it weren't for the Republican electorate,
it wouldn't make a difference.

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:59.480
Much the same way Rand Paul saying this is
unconstitutional.

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:04.260
And then when you ask him why, he says, well,
because we did it differently in the past

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:06.750
and you can file criminal charges.

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:11.990
And Trump was already embarrassed and it would
divide the country, that's not an argument

00:06:11.990 --> 00:06:13.900
about constitutionality.

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:18.190
In an in an ideal world, we would have a level
of education and media literacy such that

00:06:18.190 --> 00:06:22.800
people would be able to hear that and say
none of what Rand said has anything to do

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:25.250
with the issue of constitutionality.

00:06:25.250 --> 00:06:27.750
His arguments are invalid completely.

00:06:27.750 --> 00:06:33.550
Unfortunately, as we talked about with the
Laura Ingram case clip earlier, where she

00:06:33.550 --> 00:06:35.860
says, oh, we've now the real insurrection
is Biden.

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:40.580
And people watch and they say, yeah, yeah,
the real insurrection is Biden.

00:06:40.580 --> 00:06:46.390
The low level of media literacy makes it so
that this stuff actually has an effect and

00:06:46.390 --> 00:06:50.060
starts to sort of infect and pervade society.

00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:54.000
And it's unfortunately something that is not
easily solvable.

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:59.050
It's going to be a long time until that is
completely, completely resolved.

00:06:59.050 --> 00:07:02.770
So it's disappointing, will continue exposing
it.

00:07:02.770 --> 00:07:06.530
But beyond that, we unless we really deal
with the educational problem in this country,

00:07:06.530 --> 00:07:07.650
that's not going to get fixed.

00:07:07.650 --> 00:07:09.330
And it's a cultural problem as well.

00:07:09.330 --> 00:07:12.400
It's a combination of the two in these cultural
education.

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:15.430
It's a cultural, educational problem.

00:07:15.430 --> 00:07:20.150
And it took decades, as I've pointed out,
for it to get this bad.

00:07:20.150 --> 00:07:23.560
It's not super realistic to think in six months
